OVERVIEW. The page is a 5- and 7-place logarithmic computation that finds the root of one transcendental equation. Everything uses Briggs logarithms written with characteristic +10, so 9.xxxxx means log x − 10. An 'n' marks a negative quantity. The constant 8,2418774 is log(π/180), used to convert degrees to radians.

THE EQUATION. Each trial pair of angles satisfies β = 2α − 90°: (56°,22°), (54°,18°), (70°,50°), (65°,40°), (60°,30°), (57½°,25°), (54°40′,19°20′), (54°38′,19°16′), (54°39′,19°18′), (54°42′,19°24′), (54°41′,19°22′).

The columns of each block are:
- log sin α, log cos α;
- log sin β, log cos β;
- log 2 sin α and log 2 cos β.

The log of the number in degrees is that of 90°+α: 146, 144, 160, 155, 150, 147.5, 144.667, 144.633, 144.65, 144.7, 144.683.

Riemann then forms two quantities:
- R = (π/180)(90+α)·(5 − 2 sin α − sin β), computed as the antilog of log(90+α) + log(π/180) + log(5 − 2 sin α − sin β);
- L = 10 cos α + 2 cos β.

He tabulates the difference F(α) = L − R:
- 56°: 7.4463 − 7.562 = −0.116;
- 54°: 7.7799 − 7.7232 = +0.0567;
- 70°: 4.706 − 6.575;
- 60°: 6.732 − 7.247 = −0.515;
- 57½°: 7.1857 − 7.442 = −0.26;
- 65°: 5.7602 − 7.044.

(Mine) Substituting β = 2α − 90° gives sin β = −cos 2α and cos β = sin 2α. Writing α = 2ψ − 90° turns the equation into
10 sin 2ψ − 2 sin 4ψ − 2ψ(5 + 2 cos 2ψ − cos 4ψ) = 0.

ROOT FINDING.
- Linear interpolation between 54° (+0.0567) and 56° (−0.116) gives a step of 0.0567/0.172 ≈ 0.33 of 2°, i.e. α ≈ 54°40′.
- At 54°40′: R = 7.6690 and L = 7.6705, so F = +0.0015. An earlier evaluation, giving −0.0023 and the provisional estimate 54°38.44′, is struck out.
- He then switches to 7-place tables:
  - 54°38′: L = 7.676056, R = 7.671709, F ≈ +0.0043;
  - 54°39′: L = 7.673298, R = 7.670347, F = +0.002951;
  - 54°42′: L = 7.665022, R = 7.666297, F ≈ −0.00127 (written −0.001269);
  - 54°41′: L = 7.667781, R = 7.667643, F = +0.000138.
- Final interpolation: 138/1407 = 0.098 (side division 13800 − 12663 = 1146 → 9.8), so α = 54°41.098′.

AUXILIARY ANGLES.
- 90° − α = 35°18′54.12″, which is halved to γ = 17°39′27.06″ = 17°39.451′.
- ψ = α + γ = 45° + α/2 = 72°20.549′.
- log sin γ = 9.4819113, so sin γ = 0.303327.

INTERPRETATION (mine, verified numerically). sin ψ = 0.952887 and cos ψ = sin γ = 0.30333. This is the classical eccentricity e = 0.95289, with axis ratio c/a = √(1−e²) = 0.3033. That is where the Maclaurin spheroid sequence becomes dynamically unstable (overstable), and where Riemann's S-type ellipsoid families branch off. So the page is the numerical determination of that critical Maclaurin spheroid for Riemann's 1861 ellipsoid memoir; the bundle is 'R.Ellipsoid'.

THE FIRST BLOCK (top left) is an abandoned trial at α = 50°, with β = 10° (log sin 10° = 9.23967, log cos 10° = 9.99335) and 90 + α = 140. He began with log 50 + log(π/180) and struck that out.
